<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">École française d'Extrême-Orient</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1900">1900</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as French School of the Far East, EFEO</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The École française d'Extrême-Orient is a French institute for the study of Asian civilisations, established in 1900 with its headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> and since the 1950s in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Its scholars studied the languages, religions and archaeology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> and beyond, and it directed the clearing and restoration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> from 1908. It continues to run research centres across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ecole-francaise-dextreme-orient.jpg" alt="École française d'Extrême-Orient" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Grenouille vert · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EFEO_Paris_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
